WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.373
We will now talk about the breakpoint graph,

00:00:03.373 --> 00:00:08.030
the workhorse of genome
rearrangement studies.

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:15.760
Let's take a look at two genomes, a red
genome P, and a blue genome Q.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.380
And now let's arrange the black edges of Q
in the same order they

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:27.320
are arranged in genome P.
So they will be arranged as

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.850
+a -b -c +d.
And how the genome Q will

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:38.470
look like in the case of this new
arrangement of its black edges?

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.405
We do not change genome Q.
We just show it differently.

00:00:41.405 --> 00:00:48.280
So after we look at genome Q:
so, after a we should go to c.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.130
That's what we do.
After c, we should go to b.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:56.110
After b, we should go to -d, and
after -d, we should go to a.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.400
So that's a new representation of Q, where
black edges are

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:06.540
arranged exactly in the same way as they
are arranged in P.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.517
As soon as we've done it, we can
superimpose genome P with genome Q because

00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:14.914
black edges are arranged identically, and
we will

00:01:14.914 --> 00:01:18.396
get something that is called the
breakpoint graph.

00:01:18.396 --> 00:01:22.708
It's absolutely unclear yet what is the
value breakpoint graph,

00:01:22.708 --> 00:01:27.410
so let's learn a few things about the
breakpoint graph.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:30.921
So the first question, red and black edges

00:01:30.921 --> 00:01:34.630
in the breakpoint graph...
what do they correspond to?

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.650
Of course, they correspond to genome P,
because breakpoint

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.720
graph is simply obtained by superimposing
of P and Q.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.800
What about blue and black edges?
What do they correspond to?

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.580
Of course, they correspond to genome Q.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.540
And now we ask a strange question, what
about red and blue edges?

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.679
What do they correspond to?

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.200
And here, what happens when we limit our
attention to

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.740
red and blue edges, and they do not
corresponding to anything.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.856
So a thing that you may notice, however,
is that

00:02:13.856 --> 00:02:20.010
red and blue edges form alternating red-blue
cycles in the breakpoint graph.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:20.760
Why?

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.940
Because you may notice that at every node,
there is

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:28.490
a single red edge and a single blue edge
meeting.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.556
And therefore the resulting graph must
consist of

00:02:31.556 --> 00:02:37.279
alternating red-blue cycle,
after every red edge,

00:02:37.279 --> 00:02:39.560
there is a blue edge, and after every blue
edge, there is a red edge.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:45.205
We'll pay attention to one important
parameter of

00:02:45.205 --> 00:02:49.390
the breakpoint graph, which is the cycle
number.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.635
The cycle number is simply the number of

00:02:51.635 --> 00:02:56.210
red-blue alternating cycles in the
breakpoint graph.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:03:00.464
Why do we care about the cycle number?
But before I explain this,

00:03:00.464 --> 00:03:04.286
let's try an example,
let's construct

00:03:04.286 --> 00:03:07.950
the breakpoint graph for these two
genomes, P and Q.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:13.006
P consists of a single chromosome and Q
consists of 2 chromosomes.

00:03:13.006 --> 00:03:16.390
The first thing we do, we arrange black
edges of

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:21.730
Q in the same order they are present in
genome P.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:27.030
So it will be this arrangement of black
edges in Q.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.750
And then we will have to represent genome
Q in this order.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.680
How do we do this?

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.680
Well, we start from a.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.870
After a, we know that
we should go to -c.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.960
Afterwards we go to -f.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:47.570
Afterwards, we go to -e, and finally
we return back to +a.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:52.054
And we continue the same way to show the
smaller chromosomal with just

00:03:52.054 --> 00:03:57.474
two synteny blocks, right here.
As soon as we represent it, genome Q, in

00:03:57.474 --> 00:04:03.360
this way, we can superimpose P and Q, and
we get the breakpoint graph.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.988
And after we remove black edges from the
breakpoint graph, we see that the cycle

00:04:08.988 --> 00:04:14.520
number of P and Q is equal to 3, because
there are three cycles in this graph.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.080
Now,

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.090
given genome P, I want to ask you, what

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:25.170
is the genome Q that maximizes the cycle
number?

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:31.350
Well, the cycle number will be maximized
if every cycle will be small.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:37.550
And the shortest cycle consist of just two
edges, one red edge and one blue edge.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:42.850
Which means this is the breakpoint graph,
with the maximum number of

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.400
alternating cycles.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.590
And of course, this breakpoint graph
corresponds to the

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:52.480
case when genome Q and genome P are
identical.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.870
And the number of cycles in this graph is

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:59.700
of course simply the number of blocks in
genome P.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.860
So in this case, the number is 4.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:08.075
So it's important to realize that genome
rearrangements affect

00:05:08.075 --> 00:05:09.920
red-blue cycle.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.980
Every two breaks makes its mark on the
breakpoint graph.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:21.540
And we know that starting from the
breakpoint graph between genome

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:26.972
P and Q, doesn't matter what series of
rearrangement nature takes.

00:05:26.972 --> 00:05:29.950
That will be the breakpoint graph of two

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:33.060
identical genomes, and we know how it
looks like.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.950
It looks like the identity breakpoint
graph with the maximum

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:43.000
number of cycles.
And we will explore this fact, despite

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.260
the fact that the series of 2-break
transforming P into Q is unknown,

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:53.110
we know the number of cycles between P and
Q, in this case 2,

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:58.180
must change into the maximum number of
cycles between P and Q,

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.910
which in this case is 4.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:06.393
And this is just an example of one
possible scenario for rearrangement of

00:06:06.393 --> 00:06:12.470
genome P into genome Q, but doesn't matter
what this scenario is.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:18.700
It always will be the change from
cycle(P,Q) to the maximum number of

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:23.837
cycles in the graph.
And armed with this observation,

00:06:23.837 --> 00:06:27.929
we are ready to prove the 2-break theorem
that will

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:33.453
prove to be very important for our analysis of
the random breakage model.

